00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.680
And so here, I have
to speak with you

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.770
about a handful of
strategies that we think

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.587
we can actually help with.

00:04:37.587 --> 00:04:38.420
And I'll start here.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.810
This is something that I hear
from every single customer

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.640
that I talked to in
2019, a little bit

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.750
last year, last year was
a lot about hybrid cloud,

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.550
trying to get to a
hybrid cloud point.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.220
And maybe 10 years
before that, it

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.410
was just about getting
to cloud at all.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.230
But today, every time I
talk to a technical leader

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.800
of any kind in any organization,
the question really comes up,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.420
yeah, Google Cloud is great.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:07.190
But how do these solutions
fit into a truly multi cloud

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.770
architecture?

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.390
And I think we can look
at it and start here.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.270
And we can think
about hybrid cloud

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.600
as the bridge between
using a public cloud

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.490
and a private cloud.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.280
But multi cloud is that
and an extra dimension,

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.590
which means actually working
with multiple public clouds,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.250
public clouds that are, in
many cases, competitive.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.220
And that's actually
essential to one

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:36.170
of the tenets of the
multi-cloud architecture.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.950
In this case, last
year at Google Next,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:44.870
Google took the remarkable
step of bringing GKE on prem

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.910
into the enterprise data center.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.430
And now we have quite
a number of customers

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:51.590
up and running with
Google Cloud technology

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:56.090
running inside of their
private cloud environments.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.650
And the response
has been tremendous.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.150
And that's just
the starting point.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.733
That's really just
the starting point.

00:06:01.733 --> 00:06:04.490
And I think we'll get
into what that means.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.240
But before we go there,
I want to double down

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.280
on Twinkle's display of
the tree, the decision

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.520
tree as to why go multi-cloud.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.540
And I've boiled these
down into five or six

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:22.520
points, five or six
bullets, every one

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:26.660
of which we've got
tremendous experience helping

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.440
our customers solve one
or more of these problems.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.190
So why multi-cloud?

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:34.820
That might be on
some people's minds.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.220
Maybe if you're
here already, you're

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.740
interested in how
Google helps with this.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.010
But let me just make sure
that we start with a taxonomy,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:47.030
with a set of reasons
and motivations

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.960
to ensure that we're
on the same page.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.510
And I'll start at the
top, being able to utilize

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:54.440
more capabilities.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.390
Not every cloud has every
feature as every other cloud.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:02.000
And in some cases,
you're using one cloud

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.960
for a particular
feature or two, and then

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:07.950
another cloud for another.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:12.255
Now you could take a least
common denominator approach

00:07:12.255 --> 00:07:13.880
or greatest common
denominator approach

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:15.860
and ensure that you're
using the greatest

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.050
common aspects of
each cloud so that you

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.730
have perfect portability.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.040
And that makes sense
for some workloads.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.570
There are other
workloads, though,

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.840
that you may want
to really focus in

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.320
on a particular offering.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.510
Maybe you've got some
specialized AI ML work,

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.630
or you need a very specialized
database, or something else,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:36.170
OK?

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.380
So being able to really tap
into the unique characteristics

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:42.170
of a cloud might
be one reason why

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.030
you need to use
more than one cloud,

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.800
because maybe your
first cloud didn't

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:50.240
have one aspect or another.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.250
The second is access
to more geographies.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:55.400
And there are lots of reasons
why you might need access

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.330
to another geography.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.440
Some of the customers
I talked to, especially

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.410
in the gaming
industry, need to have

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.480
their data really close to
the users playing those games.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:07.160
It's a latency.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.550
It's a law of physics
problem at that point.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:16.820
Shaving down to milliseconds
or microseconds of latency

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:21.960
is important in the AR,
VR, augmented reality,

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.790
virtual reality world, or
some of the gaming world.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:25.790
But it's not just there.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:28.610
It's also in banks and fintech.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:33.500
We see data that cannot move
from one geography to another.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.669
And all of these are reasons
why a multi-cloud approach might

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:39.039
actually be the right answer.

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:40.700
In some cases, it's
a private cloud

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:42.620
running in certain geographies.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.920
In other cases, it's a matter
of using one or more clouds

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.660
to supplement those
geographies, being

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.600
able to ensure that you can
run those workloads in a place

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.970
where one of the public clouds
doesn't exist or doesn't have

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.080
a presence.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.500
Now the data gravity
problem is also one

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.200
that we hear quite a bit about.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.680
And we see the data gravity
problem across all industries.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.110
I'm working with a couple
of oil and gas companies

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:13.400
where this is really what it's
all about, same with the auto,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.570
and the self-driving
car industry.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.380
There's so much data
that is resident

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.240
in one cloud or another.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:22.670
Meanwhile, there's
other work that

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:25.520
happens in a different
cloud or on prem or in a car

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.550
or on a oil rig
or somewhere else.

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:31.460
And yet, crunching that
data close to that data

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.110
is essential to these
algorithms even working.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.900
And so having a multi-cloud
strategy to solve the data

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:39.680
gravity problem.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.240
Your data is here,
but something else

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:45.680
needs to happen somewhere else
is another driver for going

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.510
multi-cloud.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.850
Reducing vendor
dependency comes in a lot

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.100
of different shapes and forms.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.060
In some cases, it's a matter of
having the ability to negotiate

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.010
on prices, buy in bulk.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:09:59.830
Although, that goes both ways.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:02.150
Sometimes it's better to
go all in on one cloud

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:08.540
and get as much bulk
discount as possible.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.030
In other cases, just having
multiple vendors is something

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:13.790
that I think we've learned
over the last couple of decades

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.450
of enterprise software and
buying software as a service,

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:21.410
sourcing from multiple vendors
certainly gives the buyer

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.480
certain advantages.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.570
But it's not just that.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:31.190
It's also reducing the risk
around one vendor failing

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:31.880
or going down.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.160
Spreading a workload across
multiple clouds and load

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:35.600
balancing across,
that's something

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.430
that I've worked with
a number of startups

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:42.230
that grow to a certain
size and then become pretty

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.870
essential, too big
to fail, especially

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.400
in the transportation
and the financial space.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.880
In which case, you see a
disaster recovery or a failover

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.330
scenario with a given workload
or a database being replicated

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.640
across multiple clouds.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:01.010
It's something that
when we get to the demo,

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:04.490
Justin will touch on for sure.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:06.920
And that resilience to
failure is baked into that.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.490
So it's reducing the
vendor dependency, but also

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.820
the dependency on the
things that aren't human,

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.090
the machinery underneath,
the network connections,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.190
the distributed denial
of service attack

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:24.270
that might hit one vendor
harder than another vendor,

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.990
or at different times perhaps.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:31.160
And then the last one is
really just as an exercise

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:36.240
in large industry, the result
of mergers and acquisitions.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.330
You buy a company,
and that company

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.660
comes with its own dependencies,
its own preferences

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:42.920
for lots of things.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:46.550
Maybe it's the brand
of office equipment

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.770
and chairs in conference rooms.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.440
But that company also comes
with its own preferences

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:55.720
around software as a
service and public cloud

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.560
or private cloud as a service.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.970
There's a lot of nuance,
a lot of nuance in each

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:05.410
and every one of these.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:10.490
I'd ask if to please raise your
hand if at least one of these

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.240
did not strike a nerve with
why you would go multi-cloud.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:20.030
OK, I've successfully gotten
no one to raise their hand.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:24.380
That's a little anticlimactic.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:29.130
OK, so now we're going to
move into a second segment

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.900
of this talk.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.780
And I'm going to call
this the chalk talk.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.760
I actually created
these slides while I

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.210
was watching March Madness,
the NCAA basketball tournament.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.860
And I love basketball.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.230
And I'm always inspired
by a coach that'll

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:47.220
