and really get down

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:51.560
with the team and coach,
coach from the bench.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:55.450
OK, so now we're
into chalk talk.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.060
And we're going to start
with a couple of formulas.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.177
We're going to do this
as a math chalk talk.

00:13:00.177 --> 00:13:01.760
So this isn't a
basketball chalk talk.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:03.700
This is a math talk chalk talk.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:06.130
Maybe I'm talking more
to the tech audience

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.718
about math than basketball.

00:13:07.718 --> 00:13:08.260
I don't know.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.480
I think I cross both of those.

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:12.970
So the formulas start here.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:15.130
We're going to start
from here and build up.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.470
Containers is
something that I don't

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.835
need to explain to
this crowd anymore.

00:13:19.835 --> 00:13:22.210
Before five years ago, I think
we would've had to have a,

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:23.530
where did containers come from?

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.647
Was Docker first or
Solaris Zones first?

00:13:26.647 --> 00:13:28.980
But I think this is all well
understood, well understood

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.025
at this point.

00:13:30.025 --> 00:13:31.400
And when it comes
to multi-cloud,

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:32.830
there's one thing
that containers

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.380
gives you that's absolutely
essential to everything

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:37.750
you want to do in a
multi cloud environment.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.820
And that's portability,
the ability

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.420
to move your code, your
workload, your data from one

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.660
place to another physical
or virtual or otherwise

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.820
from on prem to
the cloud and back

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:53.320
and forth from your developer
laptop in the coffee shop

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:55.750
pushing to the build
system pushing to cloud A,

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.170
cloud B, cloud C. When
you fire one vendor

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.510
and you buy another vendor,
being able to move that.

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:05.000
And containers are
at the heart of that.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.820
And yes, virtual
machines have always

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:10.480
been able to sort of
move between clouds.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:14.560
But it's really containers
that got the concept

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:20.110
and boiled it down to its
simplest and most critical

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:23.440
definition, which is an
application, some code that you

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:27.190
bought or you wrote that bundles
its dependencies and everything

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:28.270
it needs to run.

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:31.660
And because it's in that
atomic unit as an image,

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:34.950
it can now move anywhere.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:36.820
And it was containers
and Docker containers

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:38.995
that really democratized
that technology

00:14:38.995 --> 00:14:41.283
and put that into the
hands of everyone.

00:14:41.283 --> 00:14:42.700
There were a lot
of companies that

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:44.770
were doing container and
container like things

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:46.330
for decades.

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:50.950
But this really this tectonic
shift in the landscape

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:54.790
happened four or five
years ago, about 2014.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:59.430
Now once you have a
lot of containers,

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:01.920
like containers to
the containers power,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:04.120
right, you've now got a problem.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.990
You've got a lot more machines
to manage than you ever

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:09.900
had in the physical
world, more things

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.980
to configure than you ever
had in the virtual world.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:18.870
It's really an exponential,
truly an exponential problem.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:23.010
And it's Kubernetes that
happened about that same time

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:26.010
four or five years
ago that it actually

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:29.460
provided proper tooling
to be able to handle

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:34.350
that exponentially more
complex scale and scope

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:38.590
that containers introduced
into your enterprise.

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:41.010
I've certainly spoken with
a number of executives

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.980
who now look back on that
moment when they realized

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:49.030
they had this problem of
sprawl, sprawl of code,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:51.990
maybe there were licensing
issues or support issues

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:55.140
or otherwise, and now
being able to manage that

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.140
from a single place
where Kubernetes came in

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.540
and helped with a lot of that.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.310
Now I think you
know Kubernetes is

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:08.070
built on decades of experience
at Google managing umpteen

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:11.910
illions of containers
over the years on Borg

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.490
as jobs as persistent services.

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:16.145
And we've made that
available, made

00:16:16.145 --> 00:16:17.520
that available to
the open source

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:21.300
community in the CNCF, the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation,

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.660
but certainly as a product
from Google as well.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.700
And that's the Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:30.330
Now as I alluded to
earlier, last year,

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:33.690
as we tackled the
hybrid cloud problem,

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:38.100
we brought GKE, Google
Kubernetes Engine, which we've

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:43.530
run as a hosted service, managed
service inside of Google Cloud.

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:47.940
We ported that to run inside
of the private data center.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:53.550
Anywhere that you have a vSphere
with the appropriate number

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.120
of physical and
virtual machines,

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:59.910
we can stand up a Google
Kubernetes Engine on prem, GKE

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.990
on prem inside of that
enterprise data center.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:05.490
And we're working
on extending that.

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:09.359
We're working on bringing
that to other environments

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:15.592
as well beyond our starting
point, which we've established.

00:17:15.592 --> 00:17:16.800
And it's working really well.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:19.020
But now we're starting
to gather that feedback

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.500
from those initial customers
as to where else they want

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:27.150
to take Google Kubernetes
and use that and use that

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.130
elsewhere.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.380
So now you've got a
bunch of Kubernetes,

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:32.370
a bunch of clusters.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:34.560
And inside of that,
you've successfully

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.530
deployed x to the y containers.

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:39.690
And you're scheduling and
scaling them up and down.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:40.800
And that's great.

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.320
So you've actually gone
multi-cloud at this point.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.440
But by this point in
the talk, if you've

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.340
followed and
implemented that, you've

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.300
now got Kubernetes
running in multiple places

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:52.260
and manage that.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.137
Congratulations.

00:17:54.137 --> 00:17:56.220
And you've deployed a bunch
of services into that.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.637
You haven't done that just for
the exercise of doing that.

00:17:58.637 --> 00:18:00.360
You've actually
deployed work into each

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:02.513
and every one of those.

00:18:02.513 --> 00:18:03.930
And once you've
done that, you now

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.780
have services that need
to talk to other services.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.520
You have humans that
maybe need to talk

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:09.390
to some of those services.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:11.807
But you probably have a lot
of machines and other services

00:18:11.807 --> 00:18:13.380
that need to talk
to other services.

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.770
And that's ultimately the
problem that Istio solves.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.050
Istio is part of the
Kubernetes family.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:22.890
It's another open source
project started at Google

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:26.490
that ensures that services
can talk to other services

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:27.700
and do so securely.

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:29.310
It's called a service mesh.

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:32.850
And it handles a lot of the
complexity around generating

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.190
certificates and the
certificate management,

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:40.390
the securing of services in both
directions from one to another.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:41.910
And this is a big problem.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:45.720
Without something like Istio,
distributing certificates

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.150
and ensuring that root
of trust from one service

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:52.530
to another that problem
space is ripe with a lot

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.660
of different solutions,
many of which

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:58.500
are brittle and/or
homegrown or just

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:01.110
weren't really designed
for the cloud native

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:02.880
nature of containers.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.170
But Istio was.

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:08.250
And that's exactly what we
hear from customers saying

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:10.470
that everyday they're
using to reduce

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:14.850
the complexity of communication
between those services.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.040
So Istio is often
used in conjunction

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.870
with another project,
another open source

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:20.850
project called Envoy.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:25.500
And Envoy handles the
spreading of network

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:29.760
across a bunch of services,
both inbound from a proxy

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.040
and load balancing
perspective, and, well,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.210
it's really about the
inbound at this point.

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:41.820
So being able to handle
service, those microservices

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.340
and the traffic going
to those microservices

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:47.400
and route that traffic
appropriately using

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:48.822
load balancers and tied into.

00:19:48.822 --> 00:19:50.280
Now there's a lot
of different ways

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:54.570
that you can and will use load
balancers in your clusters

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:55.770
and in your Kubernetes.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:56.970
Envoy is one of them.

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:01.140
And it's natively part
of this ecosystem.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:06.710
And these are quite useful
in handling that traffic.

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:09.370
Now serverless or
functions as a service

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:16.510
is another methodology that's
often employed in multi-cloud.

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:19.900
And Knative is another
project that we've implemented

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.040
on top of Kubernetes.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.020
So we use Kubernetes as a base.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:25.180
And then we implement
a serverless engine

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:29.860
called Knative that sits
on top of Kubernetes.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:32.710
And it can sit anywhere
Kubernetes runs.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:35.350
And what's important
here is that many

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:39.130
of the serverless
engines or feel certainly

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:41.550
like a vendor lock in.

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.190
It's something that's
native to that cloud.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:46.790
And it's something that you
want to use and you need to use.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.373
But if you're still staying true
to that multi-cloud strategy,

00:20:49.373 --> 00:20:51.520
you might be caught
in a bit of a bind.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:55.990
But Knative is how
you can actually

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:59.920
continue to use functions as a
service or a serverless engine

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:04.245
and avoid that vendor lock in.

00:21:04.245 --> 00:21:05.620
This is important
to staying true

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:11.620
to that multi-cloud strategy.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:15.700
Now part and parcel to
every single containerized

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.830
environment, especially
in the multi-cloud,

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:21.520
is a good CI/CD system,
continuous integration

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.290
continuous development.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:25.870
Ensuring that your developers
can write some code,

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:29.350
build that code,
test that, and deploy

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:32.020
that across multiple
clouds, I mean,

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.150
you've just blown out
your matrix, right?

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:37.000
Now that you have to be able
to run that same code anywhere,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.550
you need to ensure that you can
test that same code anywhere.

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:42.307
Generally, containers will
handle the portability.

00:21:42.307 --> 00:21:44.140
And it'll ensure that
you can move your code

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:45.730
from one place to another.

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:48.430
But you need to be able
to very quickly detect

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.170
where someone
somewhere has baked

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.830
in an unexpected dependency on
some thing that may not exist

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:56.030
somewhere else at some point.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:22:00.040
And that's where something
like Cloud Build or any

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:03.880
of a number of CI/CD,
continuous integration

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:07.420
continuous development,
platforms on top of Kubernetes

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.660
can help with that.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:11.500
And it's all baked very
nicely into Kubernetes,

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:13.992
which handles the scheduling
of the new container that

00:22:13.992 --> 00:22:15.700
builds your code and
maybe runs your code

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:19.210
and then spits out
that those test results

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:24.520
into a comprehensive view.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:29.340
And speaking of view, it's hard
to talk about multiple clouds

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:33.420
without at least thinking about
what a single pane of glass

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:34.420
might look like.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:36.840
So ensuring that you have
a single pane of glass

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:42.030
across multiple estates, perhaps
on prem in one cloud, cloud

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:46.650
two, cloud three, and in
your own private clouds

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:49.260
is also something that
we hear all the time is

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:53.050
just fundamental to implementing
a multi-cloud strategy.

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:54.100
And so we have that.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:56.340
It's called the
Google Cloud console.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:58.560
And from that Google
Cloud console,

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.740
which Justin will certainly show
you, from that single Google

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.680
Cloud console, you can
see multiple clusters.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.540
You can access those clusters.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:10.590
You can deploy workloads
and destroy workloads

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:14.400
and scale those workloads
all from a single UI.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.560
And it's a graphical
user interface.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:20.070
But there are good command
line tools and APIs certainly

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.240
for every one of those.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.790
That's just one of our
guiding principles.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:27.210
And this all works
because that Cloud Console

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:31.740
aggregates all of the
information about your clusters

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:32.700
into a hub.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:37.410
We call that the GKE hub.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:39.980
And it's because inside
of every cluster,

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:43.710
there is an agent that
authenticates with your account

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:47.070
and communicates with
that hub and provides

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.470
the information, the control
information, the metadata

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:52.710
information about that
cluster, so that the console

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.670
can render that for you.

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:57.760
It also catches,
receives the messages

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:01.200
that you post there asking
that cluster to do something.

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.353
So it's bi-directional.

00:24:03.353 --> 00:24:04.770
It's bi-directional
communication.

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:08.490
And you're able to do that
with a single unified view

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:13.470
across your entire estate
anywhere in the world.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:15.120
And one of the
things that I think

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:19.500
we see more used than anything
else inside of that single pane

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:24.480
of glass is the log aggregation,
the monitoring of all

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.650
of the clusters anywhere.

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:29.250
We provide some really
first class monitoring

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:32.490
and logging solutions in
Google Cloud with Stackdriver.

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:35.730
And we integrate with
a number of others.

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:41.490
You can certainly run your own
ELK Stack, Kibana, Elastic.

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:44.070
And you can run your own
Prometheus or Grafana.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.230
And many of those run
natively, all of those

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.330
run natively as containers
inside of Kubernetes.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:49.747
So once you have
a Kubernetes, you

00:24:49.747 --> 00:24:52.500
can deploy those as
workloads inside and then

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.530
use those to monitor.

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:56.820
And so being able to do
that with any cluster

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:01.290
anywhere, aggregate that
and process those logs,

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:05.340
trigger on failure,
learn from those logs

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.530
and train your
models so that you've

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:12.570
got predictive ways of avoiding
problems in the future,

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:17.660
but doing that across multiple
clouds is quite remarkable.

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:20.760
And that's something that we'll
see here in just a second.

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:27.450
And then managing those clouds,
and especially those clusters,

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:29.850
managing the configuration
across those clusters,

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:34.080
being able to
comprehensively push out

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:39.150
a security policy across many
systems, and combine those

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:43.860
into new and interesting
and secure ways

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:47.160
is essential, and
just really part

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:49.800
of ensuring that you've got
a multi-cloud story that

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:52.110
is secure, and that you're
able to roll out an update

00:25:52.110 --> 00:25:54.270
to that policy everywhere.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:58.560
So config management is
also part of this platform

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:00.690
that I'm describing.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:05.880
And I'll leave this last
one before we bring this

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:07.600
all together.

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:10.590
We've also got something
called the GKE Marketplace.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:13.500
And it's a marketplace of
solutions, software solutions,

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:16.680
containers ultimately
that can run originally

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:21.420
in your GKE clusters, the
clusters that you run inside

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:22.998
of Google Cloud.

00:26:22.998 --> 00:26:25.290
That marketplace and all of
those solutions, and that's

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:28.500
a factorial not an exclamation
point, all of those solutions

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:30.240
there come together.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:31.860
And you can deploy those.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.540
They're open source,
third party solutions.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:36.570
There's commercial software.

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:38.790
There's Google provided
software in that marketplace.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.760
You can deploy those in
new and interesting ways

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:47.410
on prem, in Google Cloud,
and now in other clouds.

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:49.660
And this is all
of these together,

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.710
the sum total is
ultimately the thing

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:55.320
that hopefully you heard
about this morning, which

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:56.550
we're calling Anthos.

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.430
Anthos being a new platform.

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:04.430
It's truly a platform that
crosses hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:08.250
This is how you actually solve
the hybrid and multi-cloud

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:12.380
problem, modernizing your
applications as containers,

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:16.620
enforcing the security
around the policy,

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:20.340
and doing so at scale
on premises in our cloud

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:21.700
and in other clouds.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:25.230
That's what we've set
out to build here.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.780
Graphically, it looks
something like this.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:31.260
I touched on a lot
of these hexagons

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:35.940
over the course of our
little math tutorial here.

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:38.790
But this is how we're seeing
this ecosystem evolve.

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:42.180
This is the
combination of services

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:45.930
necessary to bring
together a platform that

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:49.140
moves from one place
to another and has

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:53.190
that persistent experience.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:56.960
And with that, I think I'll
leave us with just a thought

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:01.970
that the ethos of
Anthos is that it's

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:06.230
a common language
across any cloud.

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:09.090
It's how things move from
one place to another.

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.040
And in that way,
it's a lot like math.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.762
Math is a common language
no matter where you're from.

00:28:13.762 --> 00:28:14.345
It's constant.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:17.930
It's consistent.

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:21.300
It's something that
we can all relate to.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.110
We don't get to change it.

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:26.360
It's not something that we
get to have opinions about.

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:28.040
There's facts.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:29.900
And that's what we've
set out to build,

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:35.430
a common language that
operates across clouds.

